Skipping the Codex approach gate: with those four answers the design space has collapsed to one obvious implementation, which is the gate's explicit skip condition. Here's the design.

## Design

**Architecture.** One new file, `settings.js`, at the repo root next to `app.js` (not under `src/` — that's the unrelated CommonJS Node entry point and shares nothing with the browser code). It owns two tables and one resolution step:

```js
// API host per environment. Resolved at load time from the page's hostname so a
// single set of files can be served to every environment without a build step.
const ENVIRONMENTS = {
  local:      { apiBaseUrl: "http://localhost:3000" },
  staging:    { apiBaseUrl: "https://api-staging.example.com" },
  production: { apiBaseUrl: "https://api.example.com" },
};

const HOSTNAME_ENVIRONMENTS = {
  "localhost": "local",
  "127.0.0.1": "local",
  "staging.example.com": "staging",
};

function resolveEnvironment(hostname) {
  return HOSTNAME_ENVIRONMENTS[hostname] || "production";
}

const environment = resolveEnvironment(window.location.hostname);

window.APP_SETTINGS = {
  environment,
  apiBaseUrl: ENVIRONMENTS[environment].apiBaseUrl,
};
```

Unknown hostnames fall through to `production` deliberately: an unrecognized host should talk to the real API rather than silently to a teammate's laptop or to staging. Switching environments is then a one-line edit to `HOSTNAME_ENVIRONMENTS`, and adding one is a two-line edit.

**Files touched — three changes, all small.**

1. **`settings.js`** (new) — as above.
2. **`index.html`** — add `<script src="settings.js"></script>` immediately before the existing `app.js` tag.
3. **`app.js`** — `API_ENDPOINT` keeps its name and its place at the top of the file; only its right-hand side changes to `` `${window.APP_SETTINGS.apiBaseUrl}/login` ``. Nothing inside `login()` changes, so the diff stays two lines.

**The one real risk: load order.** `app.js` reads `window.APP_SETTINGS` at top level, so if the script tags are ever reordered the app gets `undefined/login` — a URL that looks plausible in a log and fails confusingly at request time. `app.js` will guard with an explicit throw (`"settings.js must load before app.js"`) rather than letting the bad URL propagate. That guard is the reason this isn't purely a copy-paste move.

**Behavior is unchanged in production.** `login()` is still a stub that doesn't POST, so there's nothing to regress; on any host other than the three mapped ones, `API_ENDPOINT` resolves to exactly today's `https://api.example.com/login`.

**Testing.** The repo has no test runner and no `scripts` in `package.json`, so verification is manual: open `index.html` and confirm `APP_SETTINGS.environment` is `local` and the composed URL is correct, then check that `resolveEnvironment` returns `production` for an unmapped hostname. Which brings up the tooling question below.

**Assumption to validate:** `http://localhost:3000`, `https://api-staging.example.com`, and `staging.example.com` are placeholders — I inferred them, you didn't give them to me. Only `https://api.example.com` comes from your actual code. I'll mark these in the spec as `Assumption: ..., validate via ...` and you can overwrite them.

